What Your Electrolyte Levels Could Be Telling You
Electrolytes are small but essential players in maintaining normal body function.
They are minerals with an electrical charge that are present in body fluids. Key examples include sodium, potassium, chloride, calcium, magnesium, phosphate, and bicarbonate. Their responsibilities include maintaining fluid distribution, supporting nerve impulses, enabling muscle contractions, regulating acid-base balance, and helping maintain a normal heart rhythm.
Understanding Electrolyte Imbalance
When electrolyte concentrations move outside their normal range, an electrolyte imbalance occurs.
The effects can vary significantly. Doctors consider the laboratory value, the speed at which the change occurred, its cause, other health conditions, and the individual's age.
A mild abnormality may not cause symptoms. A sudden or significant disturbance can be much more serious.
