Recognizing the First Clues
The early warning signs are often difficult to distinguish from everyday tiredness or illness. Possible symptoms include weakness, fatigue, headache, nausea, dizziness, thirst, irritability, poor concentration, and lower exercise tolerance.
It is important not to assume that these symptoms simply mean you need more water or electrolytes. Prolonged diarrhea, vomiting, heavy sweating, illness, and certain medications can contribute to electrolyte disturbances.
Muscle symptoms can also occur. Changes in electrolyte levels may disrupt communication between nerves and muscles, potentially causing cramps, twitching, tingling, limb heaviness, or weakness.
